WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.990
Welcome to 2 minute neuroscience, where I
simplistically explain neuroscience topics

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.250
in 2 minutes or less.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.250
In this installment I will discuss membrane
potential.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:12.300
Membrane potential refers to the difference
in electrical charge between the inside and

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.950
the outside of a neuron.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.580
This is the plasma or cell membrane of the
neuron.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.230
It separates the inside of the cell from the
outside environment; we’ll say this is the

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:23.420
inside and this is the outside of the neuron.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.019
The difference in electrical charge develops
due to the grouping of ions on the inside

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:28.929
and outside of the membrane.

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:32.870
Ions are atoms that have either lost or gained
electrons and thus have a positive or negative

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.270
charge.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.140
There are several ions that play an important
role in the membrane potential of neurons.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.440
There are positively charged sodium ions,
represented by these blue circles and negatively

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.719
charged chloride ions, represented by these
green circles.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:49.350
When a neuron is at rest, the sodium ions
and chloride ions are more prevalent outside

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.829
of the cell.

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:54.739
There are also positively charged potassium
ions, represented by these yellow circles

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:59.730
and various negatively charged ions, often
referred to as organic anions represented

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.700
by these grey circles (anion is simply a term
for a negatively charged ion).

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.790
When a neuron is at rest, the potassium ions
and organic anions are more prevalent inside

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:09.800
the cell.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.430
At rest, the inside of the neuron is more
negatively charged than the outside, causing

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:18.410
the resting membrane potential of an average
neuron to be around -70 millivolts.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.110
One way this potential is maintained is through
a mechanism known as the sodium-potassium

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.110
pump.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.990
This is a transport protein that uses energy
to constantly pump three sodium ions out of

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:31.430
the cell while at the same time pumping two
potassium ions into the cell.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.500
Because there are more positive ions being
pumped out than in, it helps to keep the membrane

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.450
potential negative.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.880
Unlike other ions, potassium tends to move
fairly easily across the cell membrane through

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.800
ion channels, which are membrane spanning
proteins that allow ions to pass through.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.430
Potassium will pass out of the neuron until
it reaches the point where it is at an equilibrium

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.380
- when forces like diffusion aren’t pushing
it in one direction or the other.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.150
At this point, the membrane potential of the
neuron is around -65 to -70 mV, which is known

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.710
as the resting membrane potential.

